#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# positive_definite_matrix_q

#% text
# `positive_definite_matrix_q(m)` gives `true` if the square matrix `m` is positive definite: the real part of `x^H m x` is positive for every nonzero vector `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A Hermitian (for real entries: symmetric) matrix is positive definite when all eigenvalues are positive; any other matrix is tested through its Hermitian part `(m + m^H)/2`. The test is Sylvester's criterion on it: every leading principal minor is positive.

#% item
# A matrix that is not square gives `false`; entries that are not numbers leave the call unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
positive_definite_matrix_q([[2, 1], [1, 2]])
#% output
#= true

#% cell
positive_definite_matrix_q([[1, 2], [2, 1]])
#% output
#= false

#% cell
positive_definite_matrix_q([[2, 1 + im], [1 - im, 3]])
#% output
#= true

#% cell
positive_definite_matrix_q([[2., -1., 0.], [-1., 2., -1.], [0., -1., 2.]])
#% output
#= true

#% cell
positive_definite_matrix_q([[2.3, -1.2], [0.6, 3.7]])
#% output
#= true

